    "https://talenthub.mercy.edu/jobs/open-rank-professor-f00305-new-york-united-states": {
      "title": "Open Rank Professor- F00305",
      "description": "Open Rank Professor- F00305 - New York, United States Open Rank Professor- F00305 - New York, United States Kiosk mode So Take the Next Steps Open Rank Professor- F00305 New York, United States Faculty Full-time $75,000 - $102,000 Add to favorites View favorites Job Summary: The School of Social and Behavioral Sciences at Mercy University is seeking candidates for an Open Rank multiple year contract Professor in Social Work for its undergraduate Bachelor of Social Work (BSW) program. This position will support the delivery of the BSW program and contribute to the continued growth of the Social Work program at Mercy University. The Social Work program is also in the process of developing a Master of Social Work (MSW) program, and the successful candidate will contribute to program development, curriculum planning, and related initiatives as the program expands. Applicants must hold a PhD in Social Work from an accredited institution and a Master of Social Work (MSW) from a CSWE accredited program. Candidates must possess current licensure in social work and have at least two years of professional social work practice experience. Candidates should demonstrate experience teaching at the undergraduate level and a commitment to teaching excellence. Familiarity with Council on Social Work Education (CSWE) standards and program assessment processes is preferred. Candidates are encouraged to demonstrate engagement in the field through professional development, publications, research, or grant activity related to social work or closely related disciplines. We welcome applications from candidates with strong organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills and the ability to work effectively in a collaborative academic environment. Candidates must demonstrate a commitment to working within a historically Hispanic institution and a multicultural setting. Essential Job Functions & Responsibilities: Teaching, service, field education coordination, and maintaining currency in the field are primary responsibilities for this multiple year contract faculty position. The faculty will teach in the Social Work program and serve as the Practicum Education Coordinator for the BSW program and the developing MSW program. Core faculty are expected to teach four courses per semester. Courses may include social work practice, policy, research, human behavior in the social environment, and field seminar courses. Instruction may occur in person on the Dobbs Ferry and Bronx campuses, and through distance learning technology. Teaching assignments are determined by the Department Head. Preferred Skills: • Experience coordinating or supporting practicum education in a CSWE-accredited social work program. • Demonstrated ability to build and sustain partnerships with community agencies and organizations. • Experience with curriculum development and program assessment aligned with CSWE standards. • Engagement in scholarship, including publications, presentations, or grant-related work. • Experience teaching in multiple Apply Now First Name (required) 93352b84 Last Name (required) 8f30a0f0 Email (required) f7a6a68b Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign Up for Job Alerts Job Alert 7e4f47b4 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments School of Social & Behavorial Sci School of Social & Behavorial Sci Locations New York, United States New York, United States First Name (required) 0a389ad4 Last Name (required) 44429c70 Email (required) 1247bbfa Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs: Graduate Associate Dean, School of Nursing -F00630 The Graduate Associate Dean for the School of Nursing reports to the Sr. Associate Dean/Chief Nursing Officer and provides operational and academic leadership to support the school’