    "https://talenthub.mercy.edu/jobs/tech-support-specialist-new-york-united-states-9f5d30d4-f626-4d32-ad05-2973587a5c73": {
      "title": "Tech Support Specialist",
      "description": "Tech Support Specialist - New York, United States Tech Support Specialist - New York, United States Kiosk mode So Take the Next Steps Tech Support Specialist New York, United States Staff Full-time Minimum $ 44,400.00 Midpoint $ 57,713.00 Add to favorites View favorites Mercy University is where technology meets purpose. If you're a tech support professional who finds genuine satisfaction in helping people solve problems — and wants to do that work inside a university community that's committed to student success — this could be the right next step. What You'll Be Working On Serve as a primary point of contact for faculty, staff, and students seeking technical assistance across hardware, software, and network issues Diagnose, troubleshoot, and resolve incidents through the university's ticketing system, escalating complex issues to senior IT staff as needed Support the setup, configuration, and maintenance of university-owned devices, peripherals, and classroom technology Contribute to documentation of known issues, solutions, and IT processes to build a stronger internal knowledge base What You Bring Proven experience in a tech support, helpdesk, or desktop support role — in a higher education or similarly complex multi-user environment preferred Solid working knowledge of Windows and macOS operating systems, Microsoft 365, and common enterprise applications Familiarity with Active Directory, user account management, and basic networking concepts A clear, patient communication style that works equally well explaining a solution to a first-year student or a department chair An Associate's degree or higher in Information Technology, Computer Science, or a related field — or equivalent hands-on experience Comfort working a full-time schedule that may include occasional evening or weekend coverage to support Mercy's diverse student population Why Mercy University Mercy University has built its identity around expanding access to higher education for students who are often the first in their families to attend college. When the technology works, those students can focus entirely on what matters. Your work here has a direct line to that mission. Beyond the meaning of the work itself, Mercy offers competitive benefits including generous vacation and leave entitlements, retirement plan contributions above the standard rate, tuition benefits that extend to employees and eligible dependents, and real opportunities for professional development within the IT team. Ready to Apply? Submit your resume and a brief cover letter through the Mercy University careers portal. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis, so we encourage you to apply as soon as you're ready. We look forward to hearing from you. Apply Now First Name (required) 3ef72db0 Last Name (required) 1d6c9da2 Email (required) f2054e48 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign Up for Job Alerts Job Alert d430e802 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Information Technology Information Technology Locations New York, United States New York, United States First Name (required) 808a59f8 Last Name (required) 0c45e42e Email (required) fa1ebcf5 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs: Graduate Associate Dean, School of Nursing -F00630 The Graduate Associate Dean for the School of Nursing reports to the Sr. Associate Dean/Chief Nursing Officer and provides operational and academic leadership to support the school’s mission of excellence in graduate nursing education. Mercy University School of Nursing seeks a nurse practitioner (NP) with academic leadership experience who can simultaneously steward program quality, contribute to the development of new programs, cultivate meaningful clinical and academic partnerships, and contribute to 