    "https://talenthub.mercy.edu/jobs/open-rank-professor-and-clinical-director-counseling-f00268-dobbs-ferry-ny-new-york-united-states": {
      "title": "Open Rank Professor and Clinical Director, Counseling (F00268)",
      "description": "Open Rank Professor and Clinical Director, Counseling (F00268) - Dobbs Ferry, NY, New York, United States Open Rank Professor and Clinical Director, Counseling (F00268) - Dobbs Ferry, NY, New York, United States Kiosk mode So Take the Next Steps Open Rank Professor and Clinical Director, Counseling (F00268) Dobbs Ferry, NY, New York, United States Faculty Full-time $73,000 - $91,000 Add to favorites View favorites Job Summary: The School of Social and Behavioral Sciences at Mercy University invites applications for a full-time, Open Rank Professor/Clinical Director of Counseling 12-month multi-year contract position in the Department of Counseling, overseeing field experience for the School Counseling and Mental Health Counseling programs. This is an in-person position with opportunities to teach select courses online. The successful candidate will demonstrate excellence in teaching, commitment to diversity and social justice, and the ability to contribute meaningfully to graduate counseling education. Essential Job Functions & Responsibilities: Field Experience Coordination: Oversee practicum and internship placements for the School Counseling and Mental Health Counseling programs, including responsibility for ensuring students are appropriately placed in qualified training sites that meet program and accreditation standards. Teaching Responsibilities: The standard graduate faculty teaching load is 21 credits (7 courses) per academic year. This position includes a course release to support administrative and programmatic responsibilities related to the Clinical Director role. Teaching assignments will focus on field experience courses such as Practicum and Internship. Grant-Funded Initiatives: Provide opportunities for student engagement in grant-supported programs, including program development and student support services. Student Placement & Site Relations: Lead and manage the practicum and internship placement process, including working directly with students to secure and finalize appropriate field placements that align with program requirements. Maintain consistent communication with clinical sites and oversee the placement process from initial coordination through completion of the field experience. Partnership Development: Lead the development and maintenance of partnerships with PK–12 schools and mental health agencies to support School Counseling and Mental Health Counseling student placements. Identify, cultivate, and sustain new site relationships that align with program goals and ensure access to high-quality, supervised field experiences. Student Advising & Mentorship: Advise and mentor graduate students, fostering their academic and professional development. Program Development & Accreditation: Collaborate on program assessment, curriculum development, and efforts related to CACREP accreditation to maintain and enhance program quality. Every effort has been made to identify the essential functions of this position. It in no way states or implies that the responsibilities listed are the only duties required to perform in this position. The omission of specific statements of duties does not exclude them from the position responsibilities if the work is similar, related, or is an essential function of the position. Preferred Skills: Experience teaching in online and hybrid formats. Experience working with diverse student populations. Proficiency with Learning Management Systems such as Blackboard. Required Skills & Experience: A doctoral degree (Ph.D. or equivalent) in Counselor Education and Supervision from a CACREP-accredited program. Credentialing as a certified School Counselor and licensed Mental Health Counselor. Minimum of five years of field experience as a Mental Health Counselor and relevant experience as a School Counselor. Experience teaching graduate-level courses, particularly field experience courses such as School Counseling Practicum and Internship. Ability to build partnerships and support students' practicum and internship placements. Commitment to fostering an inclusive and multicultural learning environment. Educational Requirements: A doctoral degree (Ph.D. or equivalent) in Counselor Education and Supervision from a CACREP-accredited program. Credentialing as a certified School Counselor and/or Licensed Mental Health Counselor; credentialing in both areas is preferred. Minimum of five years of relevant field experience as a School Counselor and/or Mental Health Counselor, with experience in both settings preferred. Experience teaching graduate-level courses, particularly field experience courses such as practicum and internship in School Counseling and/or Mental Health Counseling. Demonstrated ability to coordinate, manage, and support student practicum and internship placements. Ability to build partnerships with schools, agencies, and clinical sites. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, and community partners in support of program and student success. Commitment to fostering an inclusive and multicultural learning environment. Application To learn more about the Behavioral Science program and the School of Social and Behavioral Sciences, please visit https://www.mercy.edu/academics/school-social-behavioral-sciences . Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received before October 1, 2026, will be given preference. Electronic applications are required and must include a cover letter, CV, and the names and contact information of at least three references. Candidates must apply online. About Mercy Mercy University is a dynamic, independent, coeducational New York City area university that offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate degree and certificate programs in six schools: Business, Education, Health and Natural Sciences, Liberal Arts, Nursing, and Social and Behavioral Sciences. Mercy is the region's largest private, nonprofit, Minority-Serving Institution, proud to serve a student body that is highly diverse. As a federally designated Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI), Mercy University, has been ranked nationally among top colleges in the country for Hispanics by the Hispanic Association of Colleges and Universities (HACU) and by Excelencia in Education, earning their prestigious Seal of Excelencia for intentionally serving Latino students. Mercy University was further recognized by HACU as a national leader in awarding bachelor's degrees to Hispanics in several disciplines, including Psychology, Sociology, and Business. Westchester Magazine has also named Mercy the Best College in Westchester County in 2019, 2020, and 2022. Mercy is committed to offering an inclusive learning environment to help a diverse student body set a course for their future. To support its student body, Mercy has been awarded a number of federal grants which enable us to better develop and deliver education to our students. Mercy’s student body reflects the rich diversity of the New York metropolitan area. Enrollment, including full-time and part-time undergraduates and graduates, is approximately 8,800. Mercy students learn on campuses in Dobbs Ferry, the Bronx, and Manhattan as well as online. At Mercy University we strive to provide employees and their families with a comprehensive and valuable benefits package. We offer high-quality health care plans, retirement benefits, tuition discounts, flexible spending, and much more. Our Mission Mercy University is committed to providing motivated students the opportunity to transform their lives through higher education by offering liberal arts and professional programs in personalized and high-quality learning environments, thus preparing students to embark on rewarding careers, to continue learning throughout their lives and to act ethically and responsibly in a changing world. EEO Statement Mercy University is an equal opportunity employer. Mercy University actively engages in recruiting a diverse workforce and student body that includes members of historically underrepresented groups and strives to build and sustain a welcoming and supportive campus community. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, race, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, marital status, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, pregnancy, height, weight, genetic information, disability, or protected veteran status. As part of this commitment, Mercy University will ensure that persons with disabilities are provided reasonable accommodations for the hiring process. If reasonable accommodation is needed, please contact The Office of Human Resources at (914) 674-7839, Option 6 or via email at HR@mercy.edu. The posted salary range for this position is based on several legitimate, non-discriminatory factors set by the organization. Mercy is committed to ensuring equal pay opportunities for equal work regardless of gender, race, or any other category protected by federal, state, or local pay equity laws. Campus Safety Mercy University is committed to providing a safe and welcoming environment for all. Information regarding campus safety can be found here . Apply Now First Name (required) be471ae6 Last Name (required) f43cbc3d Email (required) f318c0c5 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Sign Up for Job Alerts Job Alert f2772da0 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments School of Social & Behavorial Sci School of Social & Behavorial Sci Locations Dobbs Ferry, NY, New York, United States Dobbs Ferry, NY, New York, United States First Name (required) 26dfd659 Last Name (required) 5055ee5f Email (required) 3ab2cc77 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs: Associa